  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 113 PM CDT Sat Aug 15 2026\n\nSunday Through Monday...\n\nThis timeframe will be relatively quiet, as we will be fully post \nfrontal and generally in cool/dry advection by midday Sunday. Upper \nwave and cold front will be east of the area by early Sunday \nmorning, which will effectively help flatten the ridge that was set \nup over the area the last week, which brought persistent rainfall. \nThus, this will shunt the active baroclinic zone south of the area, \nwith surface high pressure passing over through Monday. A period of \nquiet and dry weather will be seen, with near seasonal temperatures. \nMonday looks to be a beautiful day with mostly clear skies. Enjoy \nthis reprieve from the active stretch of weather that the region has \nbeen experiencing, as the pattern becomes active once again Monday \nnight through midweek. \n\nTuesday and Beyond...\n\nMonday night into Tuesday, weak ridging will pass east of the area, \npushing the surface high pressure out and resulting in increasing \nllvl southwesterly flow. Temperatures and moisture will both \nincrease on Tuesday, with best moisture moving in later in the day. \nLLJ will kick in Tuesday evening and through the night, as a quick-\nmoving upper wave approaches the area from the northwest. A \nseasonally strong cold front will accompany this wave, providing \nsufficient surface and upper level forcing for thunderstorms. A \nbrief look at model soundings during this timeframe would indicate \nthe potential for an overnight complex of thunderstorms passing \nthrough the area, as we have seen over the last week. The main \ndifference with this system is that it will be progressive, pushing \nout of the area by early-midday Wednesday and leaving us in a post-\nfrontal airmass through the remainder of the week. The best moisture \nwill move into the area ahead of the cold front Tuesday night, with \nglobal guidance indicating 1.50-2.00\" PWATs moving in. Thus, heavy \nrainfall will be on the table with these storms. It is still  \nearly, but SPC is outlining that period for a Slight, level 2,  \nsevere threat, given the pattern and environment in place.\n\nAfter this wave pushes out of the area on Wednesday, we will see \nweak zonal-northwesterly flow set up again over the region. Surface \nhigh pressure will pass through late in the week, which should bring \na period of mostly dry weather. Although, there remains some \nuncertainty, as some long term models indicate subtle waves passing \nthrough bringing low-end PoPs. NBM favors the mostly-dry forecast, \nleaving PoPs <20% through Friday. The post-frontal airmass will keep \ntemperatures cooler through the second half of the week, with most \nin the mid 70s.",
